# =============================================================================
# numerus — Numerus arithmetic is exact; a value that does not fit traps at the store.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Exact intermediates — `max + 1` is 9223372036854775808, one past the
#     largest `int`; an expression is a number, not a 64-bit container, so it
#     prints exactly and `max + 1 - 1` comes back to `max`.
#   • Trap at the store — `var int stored ← max + 1` fails because the exact
#     value does not fit `int`; the program aborts with
#     "9223372036854775808 does not fit in `i64`" and never wraps.
#   • The exact range — intermediates may reach [−2⁶³, 2⁶⁴ − 1]; past it
#     (`max * max`) the arithmetic itself traps with "numerus overflow".
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting `max + 1` to wrap to the minimum, or to trap where it is computed.
#   • Expecting the store trap to be recoverable with `fac … cape` — an implicit
#     store trap is not in the `⇥` channel; use `↦ … ⊥` for a recoverable check.
#   • Expecting numerus to wrap like modular words — use `wrapping<W>` for that.
#
# See also: +, numerus, wrapping
# =============================================================================

# numerus-overflow — exact arithmetic, checked store
#
# POLICY:
#   Bare `numerus` is i64. Integer arithmetic is exact. The policy of the
#   destination slot is applied where a value lands in it, and a trapping slot
#   aborts on a value that does not fit. No arithmetic wraps silently.

main {
    let int max ← 9223372036854775807
    print max + 1
    print max + 1 - 1
    mut int stored ← max + 1
    print stored
}
